\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe image of $u$ under $T$ is given by $Au+b.$ So, by applying $T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertices of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(0,-1),(1,0),$ and $(-2,0).$\n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/1a/1aed421f9d6458fb8b72a44c83703b3f1c0475cee9ef9ec2f0a4cfcaad74d7fc.png)\n\n          \n\nFinally, since $\\det (A)=3>0,$ this transformation preserves orientation." 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186179990 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation is already in matrix form \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ maps the boundary of $Δ$ to the boundary of the image. So, to find the image of $Δ,$ we find the images of each side.\n\n![](images/0d/0d736c2e3fa2c4ba1872b045b8bdcf4def98a24337683e368bec337b810b6acb.png)\n\n          \n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our triangle:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $b$ in each column:\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe image of $u$ under $T$ is given by $Au+b.$ So, by applying $T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertices of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(0,-1),(1,0),$ and $(-2,0).$\n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/1a/1aed421f9d6458fb8b72a44c83703b3f1c0475cee9ef9ec2f0a4cfcaad74d7fc.png)\n\n          \n\nFinally, since $det(A)=3>0,$ this transformation preserves orientation." 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186179992 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThe transformation is invertible if the matrix $A$ is invertible. That is, if $\\det (A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}v-u \\\\ u+v\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-u+v \\\\ u+v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\det (A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=(-1)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 1=-2\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{1}$ is invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{2}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-2 \\\\ u-v+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ u-v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\det (A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=1\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 1=-1\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{2}$ is invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{3}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{3}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u+6v \\\\ u+2v-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u+6v \\\\ u+2v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\det (A)=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=3\\cdot 2-6\\cdot 1=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{3}$ is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{2}$ only.\"" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186179992 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThe transformation is invertible if the matrix $A$ is invertible. That is, if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{b