ligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x$ lies in the second quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{12}{13}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{5}{13}\\right)\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{48}{65}-\\frac{15}{65} \\\\  & =-\\frac{63}{65}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186184825 1074 "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡x=1-{cos}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡x & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-{\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(-4)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x$ lies in the second quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{12}{13}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{5}{13}\\right)\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{48}{65}-\\frac{15}{65} \\\\  & =-\\frac{63}{65}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186184830 1074 "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}(x+2C))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+2C$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}(x+2C))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\tan \\,\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/8a/8a19beac86998c845d7ffa225a52d425ab48b9ec350f6c8fe42f7c6dbce027ce.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n2C=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186184830 1074 "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}(x+2C))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+2C$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}(x+2C))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=tan\\,\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/8a/8a19beac86998c845d7ffa225a52d425ab48b9ec350f6c8fe42f7c6dbce027ce.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n2C=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{