hat $u$ lies in the third quadrant? \n\nFirst, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u-v) = \\sin u \\cos v- \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{v}$, $\\cos{v}$, and $\\sin{u}.$ To find the missing value of $\\cos{u}$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 u = 1- \\sin^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\cos }^{2}⁡u & =1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{\\left(-\\frac{12}{13}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-{\\left(\\frac{12}{13}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{12}^{2}}{{13}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{144}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{169}{169}-\\frac{144}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\cos u= -\\dfrac{5}{13}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{12}{13}\\right)\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{5}{13}\\right)\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{48}{65}+\\frac{15}{65} \\\\  & =-\\frac{33}{65}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186185347 1064 "We can compute trigonometric ratios using the sum and difference formulas for sine together with the Pythagorean identity.\n\nLet's suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin v = \\dfrac{3}{5}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac{4}{5}, \\qquad \\sin u = -\\dfrac{12}{13}.\n$$\n\nHow can we find the value of $\\sin\\left(u - v\\right)$ given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant? \n\nFirst, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u-v) = \\sin u \\cos v- \\cos u \\