\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186188983 1074 "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186189359 1064 "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a distribution with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2$. Let's define a new random variable as the sum of all $X_i$'s as follows:\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nThe sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\nn\\cdot \\overline{X} =\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nTherefore, our random variable $X$ is simply the product of $n$ with the sample mean:\n\n$$\nX = n\\cdot \\overline{X}\n$$\n\nNow, the central limit theorem (CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n$, the distribution of $\\overline{X}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\sim N\\!\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, since\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline X] = \\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\overline X] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $\\textrm E[X]$ and $\\textrm{Var}[X]$ using the properties of mean and variance as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= E[nX^(―)] \\\\\n&= n \\cdot E[X^(―)] \\\\\n&= nμ \\\\\nVar[X] &= Var[nX^(―)] \\\\\n&= n^{2} \\cdot Var[X^(―)] \\\\\n&= n^{2} \\cdot \\frac{σ^{2}}{n} \\\\\n&= nσ^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\overline{X}$ is (approximately) normally distributed, we have that $X$ is also approximately normal. Therefore,\n\n$$\nX\\sim N(n\\mu, n\\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nThis is an important result. It tells us that provided that we have a sufficiently large I.I.D. sample, the sum of the elements from that sample is approximately normally distributed with mean $n\\mu$ and variance $n\\sigma^2$. This result does not depend on the distribution of the $X_i$'s!" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186189359 1064 "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a distribution with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2$. Let's define a new random variable as the sum of all $X_i$'s as follows:\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nThe sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\nn\\cdot \\overline{X} =\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nTherefore, our random variable $X$ is simply the product of $n$ with the sample mean:\n\n$$\nX = n\\cdot \\overline{X}\n$$\n\nNow, the central limit theorem (CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n$, the distribution of $\\overline{X}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\sim N\\!\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, since\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline X] = \\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\overline X] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $\\textrm E[X]$ and $\\textrm{Var}[X]$ using the properties of mean and variance as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= E[nX^(―)] \\\\\n&= n \\cdot E[X^(―)] \\\\\n&= nμ \\\\\n\\operatorname{Var} [X] &= \\operatorname{Var} [nX^(―)] \\\\\n&= n^{2} \\cdot \\operatorname{Var} [X^(―)] \\\\\n&= n^{2} \\cdot \\frac{σ^{2}}{n} \\\\\n&= nσ^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\overline{X}$ is (approximately) normally distributed, we have that $X$ is also approximately normal. Therefore,\n\n$$\nX\\sim N(n\\mu, n\\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nThis is an important result. It tells us that provided that we have a sufficiently large I.I.D. sample, the sum of the elements from that sample is approximately normally distributed with mean $n\\mu$ and variance $n\\sigma^2$. This result does not depend on the distribution of the $X_i$'s!" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186189364 1064 "Let's recall the Pythagorean trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $\\cos^2 \\theta,$ we obtain our secant-tangent identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\ 1+{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\right)}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\right)}^{2} \\\\ 1+{\\tan }^{2}⁡\\theta  & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we subtract $1$ from both sides, we isolate the $\\tan^2\\theta$ term.\n\n$$\n\\tan^2\\theta = \\sec^2\\theta - 1\n$$\n\nThis is an **alternate form** of the secant-tangent identity. \n\nWe can get a second alternate form if we again start with \n\n$$\n1 + \\tan^2\\theta = \\sec^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nSubtracting $\\tan^2\\theta$ from both sides, we get\n\n$$\n\\sec^2\\theta - \\tan^2\\theta = 1.\n$$\n\nWe can use these alternate forms to simplify trigonometric expressions. Let's see an example." 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186189364 1064 "Let's recall the Pythagorean trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $\\cos^2 \\theta,$ we obtain our secant-tangent identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\ 1+{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\right)}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\right)}^{2} \\\\ 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta  & ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we subtract $1$ from both sides, we isolate the $\\tan^2\\theta$ term.\n\n$$\n\\tan^2\\theta = \\sec^2\\theta - 1\n$$\n\nThis is an **alternate form** of the secant-tangent identity. \n\nWe can get a second alternate form if we again start with \n\n$$\n1 + \\tan^2\\theta = \\sec^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nSubtracting $\\tan^2\\theta$ from both sides, we get\n\n$$\n\\sec^2\\theta - \\tan^2\\theta = 1.\n$$\n\nWe can use these alternate forms to simplify trigonometric expressions. Let's see an example." 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186189368 1064 "It's often helpful to use the double-angle formula for sine when computing the slope of the tangent to a polar curve, as this often makes the differentiation easier.\n\nLet's consider the polar curve $r = 1-2\\cos\\theta,$ whose graph is shown below \n\n![](images/53/53c660d25a8bec2346cacd95d70fb3f4ee27afe039bd23cbe7affa3cf04eb09a.png)\n\nRecall that the slope of the tangent (in terms of $\\theta$) is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r = 1-2\\cos\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the double-angle formula $\\color{black}\\sin2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta$ to simplify our expression for $y.$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we need to calculate $x'(\\theta)$ and $y'(\\theta).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′}-(2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )-(2\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′}-{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the slope of the tangent is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\text{ }\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2\\text{ }\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186189368 1064 "It's often helpful to use the double-angle formula for sine when computing the slope of the tangent to a polar curve, as this often makes the differentiation easier.\n\nLet's consider the polar curve $r = 1-2\\cos\\theta,$ whose graph is shown below \n\n![](images/53/53c660d25a8bec2346cacd95d70fb3f4ee27afe039bd23cbe7affa3cf04eb09a.png)\n\nRecall that the slope of the tangent (in terms of $\\theta$) is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}.\n$$\n\nFirst, to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r = 1-2\\cos\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the double-angle formula $\\color{black}\\sin2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta$ to simplify our expression for $y.$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we need to calculate $x'(\\theta)$ and $y'(\\theta).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′}-(2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )-(2\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′}-{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the slope of the tangent is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\thet