 }\\left(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that substituting $\\theta = \\pi$ gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00,$ so evaluating this limit by direct substitution won't work. However, we can evaluate this limit using L'Hopital's rule.\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{(2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (0)+0}{2\\cdot 1+(-1)}