igned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, the $5\\%$ *two-tailed* critical value (the same as the\n\n$$\n5\\% \\div 2 = 2.5\\%\n$$\n\n*one-tailed* critical value) for $k = 11$ degrees of freedom is\n\n$$\nt \\approx 2.101\n$$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k \\setminus p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$10$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.372\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.812\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.228\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.764\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,3.169\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$11$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.363\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.796\\,$</td>\n<td data-fill=\"PapayaWhip\">$\\,\\color{blue}2.201\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.718\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,3.106\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$12$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.356\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.782\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.179\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.681\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,3.055\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince we are considering both tails, our critical region is\n\n$$\nT \\leq \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-2.201} \\qquad\\textrm{or}\\qquad T \\geq \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2.201}\n$$\n\nOur test statistic $(-2.520)$ lies in the critical region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/66/66df920face74df61db66b8ab8e4d9ebc454972869bb417028fd29d633222c4e.png)\n\nSo, we reject the null hypothesis $H_0$. As a result, we conclude the following:\n\n> *There is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{sufficient}}$ evidence that, at the $5\\%$ level of significance, we have $\\mu_x \\neq \\mu_y$.*" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186192528 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$. However, the sample sizes\n\n$$\nn_x = 42 \\geq 30\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn_y = 47 \\geq 30\n$$\n\nare *sufficiently large.* Therefore, we can apply the central limit theorem.\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y}- (\\mu_x - \\mu_y)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{S_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{S_y^2}{n_y}}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated using a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means,\n- $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ are the population means,\n- $n_x$ and $n_y$ are the sample sizes, and\n- $S_x^2$ and $S_y^2$ are the sample variances.\n\nIn our example:\n\n- $H_0: \\mu_x = \\mu_y$ is the null hypothesis\n- $H_1: \\mu_x < \\mu_y$ is the alternative (one-tailed) hypothesis\n\nWe can estimate the number of degrees of freedom $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk &= \\min (n_{x} - 1, n_{y} - 1) \\\\\n&= \\min (42 - 1, 47 - 1) \\\\\n&= \\min (41, 46) \\\\\n&= 41\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssuming the null hypothesis, i.e.\n\n$$\n\\mu_x - \\mu_y = 0\n$$\n\nwe compute the test statistic:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt &= (x^(―) - y^(―) - (μ_{x} - μ_{y}))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{s_{x}^{2}}{n_{x}} + \\frac{s_{y}^{2}}{n_{y}}}) \\\\\n&= (2.1 - 3.8 - (0))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{8^{2}}{42} + \\frac{3^{2}}{47}}) \\\\\n&\\approx - 1.298\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, the $5\\%$ one-tailed critical value for $k = 41$ degrees of freedom is\n\n$$\nt \\approx 1.683\n$$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"