ced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186231570 1071 "A particle of mass $0.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=(3{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186231570 1074 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.5{y}^{″}=-ky+(3{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2ky=3(2{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2t)-1).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2k\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=3(2{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2t)-1)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=3(2{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2t)-1)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $4.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186231570 1074 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.5{y}^{″}=-ky+(3{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-\\frac{3}{2}).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2ky=3(2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-1).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2k\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=3(2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-1)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=3(2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)-1)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $4.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186231572 1074 "Because $f$ is $4$-periodic on $[-2,2]$, we have $L=2$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis \n\n$$\n\\cos \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is odd, its Fourier series contains only sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a sine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186231572 1074 "Because $f$ is $4$-periodic on $[-2,2]$, we have $L=2$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis \n\n$$\ncos\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{and}\\,sin\\,\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is odd, its Fourier series contains only sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a sine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186231780 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=2{\\cos }^{2}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186231780 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=2{cos}^{2}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186231780 1074 "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\omega =4.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=2{\\cos }^{2}⁡pt$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =4.$ Since \n\n$$\nf(t)=2{\\cos }^{2}⁡pt=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2pt,\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $2p.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=\\frac{\\omega }{2}=2.$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186231780 1074 "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\omega =4.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=2{cos}^{2}⁡pt$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =4.$ Since \n\n$$\nf(t)=2{cos}^{2}⁡pt=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2pt,\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $2p.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=\\frac{\\omega }{2}=2.$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186233032 1071 "If ${\\log }_{8}⁡x+2{\\log }_{8}⁡x=2$, then what is the value of  $x?$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186233032 1071 "If ${log}_{8}⁡x+2{log}_{8}⁡x=2$, then what is the value of  $x?$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186233032 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{8}⁡x+2{\\log }_{8}⁡x & =2 \\\\ (1+2){\\log }_{8}⁡x & =2 \\\\ 3{\\log }_{8}⁡x & =2 \\\\ {\\log }_{8}⁡x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $8,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{8}⁡x & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {8}^{{\\log }_{8}⁡x} & ={8}^{2/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${8}^{{\\log }_{8}⁡x}=x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={8}^{2/3} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{64} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186233032 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡x+2{log}_{8}⁡x & =2 \\\\ (1+2){log}_{8}⁡x & =2 \\\\ 3{log}_{8}⁡x & =2 \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $8,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡x & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {8}^{{log}_{8}⁡x} & ={8}^{2/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${8}^{{log}_{8}⁡x}=x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={8}^{2/3} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{64} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186233201 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {csc}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cot}x+C, \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{sec}x+C, \\\\ \\int \\sin \\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cos}x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-2{csc}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int {csc}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\operatorname{sin}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}x-2(-\\operatorname{cot}x)+(-\\operatorname{cos}x)+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}x+2\\operatorname{cot}x-\\operatorname{cos}x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186233201 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {csc}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cot}x+C, \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{sec}x+C, \\\\ \\int sin\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cos}x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-2{csc}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int {csc}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\operatorname{sin}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}x-2(-\\operatorname{cot}x)+(-\\operatorname{cos}x)+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}x+2\\operatorname{cot}x-\\operatorname{cos}x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186233277 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186233277 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186233277 1074 "First, we rewrite the numerator of the first term using $1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡x={\\sin }^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1=\\frac{1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the numerator of the first term as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1 & =\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1}-1 \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186233277 1074 "First, we rewrite the numerator of the first term using $1-{cos}^{2}⁡x={sin}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1=\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the numerator of the first term as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1 & =\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1}-1 \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186233379 1071 "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle D=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 6 \\\\ 2x & x-2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $\\det (D)=-32$, what is the value of $x?$\n\n$x=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186233379 1071 "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle D=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 6 \\\\ 2x & x-2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(D)=-32$, what is the value of $x?$\n\n$x=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186233379 1074 "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 6 \\\\ 2x & x-2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot (x-2)-6\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-16x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det (D)=-32$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (D) & =-32 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-16x & =-32 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-16x+32 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+16 & =0 \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186233379 1074 "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 6 \\\\ 2x & x-2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot (x-2)-6\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-16x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=-32$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =-32 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-16x & =-32 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-16x+32 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+16 & =0 \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186233441 1071 "Consider the function $y=\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){\\sin }^{2}⁡x}.$  The derivative of this function for $x\\in (0,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{g(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+h(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x)+h(x).$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186233441 1071 "Consider the function $y=\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}⁡x}.$  The derivative of this function for $x\\in (0,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{g(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+h(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x)+h(x).$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186233441 1074 "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){\\sin }^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}\\cdot \\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+6x)^{1/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+6x)^{1/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+6x)^{1/2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+6x}\\cdot (2x+6)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){\\sin }^{2}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){\\sin }^{2}⁡x}(\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){\\sin }^{2}⁡x}(\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){\\sin }^{2}⁡x}\\left(\\frac{(x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\frac{(x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that $x\\in (0,\\pi ),$ then $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ so the fraction $\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ simplifies to $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x)+h(x)=x+3+{x}^{2}+6x={x}^{2}+7x+3.\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186233441 1074 "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}\\cdot \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+6x)^{1/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+6x)^{1/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+6x)^{1/2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+6x}\\cdot (2x+6)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}⁡x}(\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}⁡x}(\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}⁡x}\\left(\\frac{(x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\frac{(x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that $x\\in (0,\\pi ),$ then $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ so the fraction $\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ simplifies to $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x)+h(x)=x+3+{x}^{2}+6x={x}^{2}+7x+3.\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186233518 1071 "Which of the following is a horizontal asymptote of $y={\\log }_{6}⁡x?$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186233518 1071 "Which of the following is a horizontal asymptote of $y={log}_{6}⁡x?$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186233518 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y={\\log }_{6}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/41/4177d9520b3758b29926eba7a7a74ed4c508d861e3c343f46c372ec79500fc9d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note that for $x>1$, the graph grows very slowly as we increase $x.$ \n\nWe say that $y\\to \\infty $ (slowly) as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, there are no horizontal asymptotes." 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186233518 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y={log}_{6}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/41/4177d9520b3758b29926eba7a7a74ed4c508d861e3c343f46c372ec79500fc9d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note that for $x>1$, the graph grows very slowly as we increase $x.$ \n\nWe say that $y\\to \\infty $ (slowly) as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, there are no horizontal asymptotes." 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186233519 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y={\\log }_{6}⁡x?$ \n\nI. It has no zeros\n\nII. $y\\to 6$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n\nIII. It has a vertical asymptote at $x=0$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186233519 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y={log}_{6}⁡x?$ \n\nI. It has no zeros\n\nII. $y\\to 6$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n\nIII. It has a vertical asymptote at $x=0$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186233519 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y={\\log }_{6}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/d8/d8d3f9dc995d8801d451b15129860362efa772178cdc3ef07be5adb6a1da5c45.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function has a zero at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. As $x$ increases, $y$ increases without bound. Therefore, $y\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty $\n\n- Statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true." 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186233519 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y={log}_{6}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/d8/d8d3f9dc995d8801d451b15129860362efa772178cdc3ef07be5adb6a1da5c45.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function has a zero at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. As $x$ increases, $y$ increases without bound. Therefore, $y\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty $\n\n- Statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true." 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186233555 1074 "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded quarterly, and there are $4$ quarters in one year, so $n=4.$ We also have an initial amount of ${P}_{0}=12,000$ and annual rate of $r=0.08.$\n\nTo find the number of years it will take until $P\\ge 18,000,$ we substitute these values into the formula and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt} & \\ge 18,000 \\\\ 12,000{(1+\\frac{0.08}{4})}^{4t} & \\ge 18,000 \\\\ (1+0.02)^{4t} & \\ge \\frac{18,000}{12,000} \\\\ (1.02)^{4t} & \\ge 1.5 \\\\ \\log \\,({1.02}^{4t}) & \\ge \\log \\,(1.5) \\\\ 4t\\log \\,(1.02) & \\ge \\log \\,(1.5) \\\\ 4t & \\ge \\frac{\\log \\,(1.5)}{\\log \\,(1.02)} \\\\ 4t & \\ge 20.475… \\\\ t & \\ge \\frac{20.475…}{4}\\approx 5.12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember, payments are compounded quarterly, so we must choose either $5$ or $5.25$ years.\n\n- After $5$ years, there is still less than $\\$18,000$ in the account, because $5<5.12.$\n\n- After $5.25$ years, there will be more than $\\$18,000$ in the account, since $5.25>5.12.$\n\nTherefore, it will take $5.25$ years ($5$ years and $1$ quarter) until there is at least $\\$18,000$ in the account." 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186233555 1074 "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded quarterly, and there are $4$ quarters in one year, so $n=4.$ We also have an initial amount of ${P}_{0}=12,000$ and annual rate of $r=0.08.$\n\nTo find the number of years it will take until $P\\ge 18,000,$ we substitute these values into the formula and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt} & \\ge 18,000 \\\\ 12,000{(1+\\frac{0.08}{4})}^{4t} & \\ge 18,000 \\\\ (1+0.02)^{4t} & \\ge \\frac{18,000}{12,000} \\\\ (1.02)^{4t} & \\ge 1.5 \\\\ log\\,({1.02}^{4t}) & \\ge log\\,(1.5) \\\\ 4tlog\\,(1.02) & \\ge log\\,(1.5) \\\\ 4t & \\ge \\frac{log\\,(1.5)}{log\\,(1.02)} \\\\ 4t & \\ge 20.475… \\\\ t & \\ge \\frac{20.475…}{4}\\approx 5.12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember, payments are compounded quarterly, so we must choose either $5$ or $5.25$ years.\n\n- After $5$ years, there is still less than $\\$18,000$ in the account, because $5<5.12.$\n\n- After $5.25$ years, there will be more than $\\$18,000$ in the account, since $5.25>5.12.$\n\nTherefore, it will take $5.25$ years ($5$ years and $1$ quarter) until there is at least $\\$18,000$ in the account." 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186233646 1074 "First, we take $\\ln $ of both sides of the equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x+3} & =7 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2x+3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ 2x+3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx≈-0.527\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186233646 1074 "First, we take $ln$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x+3} & =7 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2x+3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ 2x+3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx≈-0.527\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186233679 1071 "Find $x$ such that ${\\log }_{4}⁡(3x-2)=3.$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186233679 1071 "Find $x$ such that ${log}_{4}⁡(3x-2)=3.$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186233679 1074 "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $4,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{4}⁡(3x-2) & =3 \\\\ {4}^{{\\log }_{4}⁡(3x-2)} & ={4}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${4}^{{\\log }_{4}⁡(3x-2)}=3x-2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-2 & ={4}^{3} \\\\ 3x-2 & =64 \\\\ 3x & =66 \\\\ x & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186233679 1074 "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $4,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡(3x-2) & =3 \\\\ {4}^{{log}_{4}⁡(3x-2)} & ={4}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${4}^{{log}_{4}⁡(3x-2)}=3x-2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-2 & ={4}^{3} \\\\ 3x-2 & =64 \\\\ 3x & =66 \\\\ x & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186233696 1074 "First, we take $\\ln $ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-4x} & =\\sqrt[3]{15} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}({e}^{1-4x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}(\\sqrt[3]{15}) \\\\ 1-4x & =\\operatorname{ln}({15}^{1/3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule to the right-hand side and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-4x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}(15) \\\\ -4x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}(15)-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{ln}(15)+\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx\\approx 0.024\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186233696 1074 "First, we take $ln$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-4x} & =\\sqrt[3]{15} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}({e}^{1-4x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}(\\sqrt[3]{15}) \\\\ 1-4x & =\\operatorname{ln}({15}^{1/3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule to the right-hand side and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-4x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}(15) \\\\ -4x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}(15)-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{ln}(15)+\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx\\approx 0.024\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186233697 1074 "First, we take ${log}_{9}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{2y+1} & ={5}^{-y} \\\\ {\\log }_{9}⁡({9}^{2y+1}) & ={\\log }_{9}⁡({5}^{-y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${\\log }_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\n2y+1={\\log }_{9}⁡({5}^{-y})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n2y+1=-y{\\log }_{9}⁡(5)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+1 & =-y{\\log }_{9}⁡(5) \\\\ 2y+y{\\log }_{9}⁡(5) & =-1 \\\\ y(2+{\\log }_{9}⁡(5)) & =-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2+{\\log }_{9}⁡(5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186233697 1074 "First, we take ${log}_{9}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{2y+1} & ={5}^{-y} \\\\ {log}_{9}⁡({9}^{2y+1}) & ={log}_{9}⁡({5}^{-y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\n2y+1={log}_{9}⁡({5}^{-y})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n2y+1=-y{log}_{9}⁡(5)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+1 & =-y{log}_{9}⁡(5) \\\\ 2y+y{log}_{9}⁡(5) & =-1 \\\\ y(2+{log}_{9}⁡(5)) & =-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2+{log}_{9}⁡(5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186233699 1071 "Given that ${\\log }_{2}⁡(3x+1)={\\log }_{2}⁡(5-x),$ what is the value of $x?$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186233699 1071 "Given that ${log}_{2}⁡(3x+1)={log}_{2}⁡(5-x),$ what is the value of $x?$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186233699 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{2}⁡(3x+1) & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(5-x) \\\\ 3x+1 & =5-x \\\\ 4x & =4 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{2}⁡(3(1)+1) & \\overset{?}{=}{\\log }_{2}⁡(5-1) \\\\ {\\log }_{2}⁡(4) & ={\\log }_{2}⁡(4)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is a valid solution." 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186233699 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(3x+1) & ={log}_{2}⁡(5-x) \\\\ 3x+1 & =5-x \\\\ 4x & =4 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(3(1)+1) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{2}⁡(5-1) \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(4) & ={log}_{2}⁡(4)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is a valid solution." 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186233810 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/fc/fc3602d0d057902e1a510485ae8f02746afd80ea3bb7c2c8efc40ccfaf7b3dd4.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $0<\\operatorname{arg}(z)<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arg}(z)=\\arctan \\,\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)\\approx 1.21,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186233810 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/fc/fc3602d0d057902e1a510485ae8f02746afd80ea3bb7c2c8efc40ccfaf7b3dd4.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $0<\\operatorname{arg}(z)<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arg}(z)=arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)\\approx 1.21,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186233812 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/ea/ea995ef688b12c8d58076fc832c9a5c735ff5933aff3ac6c347ae27850adbb2e.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\pi <\\operatorname{arg}(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}\\alpha  & =\\frac{6}{6\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\arctan \\,\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z)=\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186233812 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/ea/ea995ef688b12c8d58076fc832c9a5c735ff5933aff3ac6c347ae27850adbb2e.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\pi <\\operatorname{arg}(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}\\alpha  & =\\frac{6}{6\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\alpha  & =arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z)=\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186233824 1074 "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}<\\theta <{90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}(-{30}^{∘})=-\\operatorname{tan}({30}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $-{30}^{∘}$ lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\arctan \\,\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)=-{30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n4\\arctan \\,\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)=4\\cdot (-{30}^{∘})={-120}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186233824 1074 "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}<\\theta <{90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}(-{30}^{∘})=-\\operatorname{tan}({30}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $-{30}^{∘}$ lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\narctan\\,\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)=-{30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n4arctan\\,\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)=4\\cdot (-{30}^{∘})={-120}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186233882 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\pi /3}\\csc \\,(\\frac{3x}{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186233882 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\pi /3}csc\\,(\\frac{3x}{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186233882 1074 "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ directly into the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /3}\\csc \\,(\\frac{3x}{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186233882 1074 "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ directly into the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim