e that we're given that the lower limit $a=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\pi }{2n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2n}=\\frac{(b-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{l}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n}\n$$ and $f({x}_{l})={\\sec }^{2}⁡{x}_{l}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{\\sec }^{2}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={\\sec }^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186234342 1074 "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{l}=a+lΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\pi }{2n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2n}=\\frac{(b-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{l}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n}\n$$ and $f({x}_{l})={sec}^{2}⁡{x}_{l}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{sec}^{2}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186234420 1071 "Solve the equation ${\\log }_{4}⁡({x}^{2}+4)={\\log }_{4}⁡(-3x+2).$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186234420 1071 "Solve the equation ${log}_{4}⁡({x}^{2}+4)={log}_{4}⁡(-3x+2).$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186234420 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{4}⁡({x}^{2}+4) & ={\\log }_{4}⁡(-3x+2) \\\\ {x}^{2}+4 & =-3x+2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=-2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{4}⁡({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{\\log }_{4}⁡(-3x+2) \\\\ {\\log }_{4}⁡((-2)^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{\\log }_{4}⁡(-3(-2)+2) \\\\ {\\log }_{4}⁡(8) & ={\\log }_{4}⁡(8).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-2$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{4}⁡({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{\\log }_{4}⁡(-3x+2) \\\\ {\\log }_{4}⁡((-1)^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{\\log }_{4}⁡(-3(-1)+2) \\\\ {\\log }_{4}⁡(5) & ={\\log }_{4}⁡(5).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-1$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-2,-1.$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186234420 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡({x}^{2}+4) & ={log}_{4}⁡(-3x+2) \\\\ {x}^{2}+4 & =-3x+2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=-2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}⁡(-3x+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡((-2)^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}⁡(-3(-2)+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(8) & ={log}_{4}⁡(8).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-2$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}⁡(-3x+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡((-1)^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}