\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}5\\sin ⁡(2x) \\\\  & =5\\sin ⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\sin ⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186235421 1074 "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}5sin⁡(2x) \\\\  & =5sin⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot sin⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186235422 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x)=5\\sin ⁡(2x).$\n\n![](images/67/67f6ec4f72e7bd74ac9ae5fa0f195cda1e90e8d8e6fad1bf85944a3dfd17fdfb.png)\n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty $, the graph oscillates between the values $y=-5$ and $y=5$ indefinitely. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }5\\sin ⁡(2x)=DNE.\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186235422 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x)=5sin⁡(2x).$\n\n![](images/67/67f6ec4f72e7bd74ac9ae5fa0f195cda1e90e8d8e6fad1bf85944a3dfd17fdfb.png)\n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty $, the graph oscillates between the values $y=-5$ and $y=5$ indefinitely. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }5sin⁡(2x)=DNE.\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186235423 1074 "We have the function $f(x)=5\\sin ⁡(2x).$ Notice that we are given the length $h$ of the interval. So, the average rate of change of $f$ over this interval is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h},\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Substituting into the formula and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+h)-f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sin ⁡(2(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+h))-5\\sin ⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sin ⁡(\\pi +2h)-5\\sin ⁡(\\pi )}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sin ⁡(\\pi +2h)-5(0)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sin ⁡(\\pi +2h)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{h}\\sin ⁡(\\pi +2h).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186235423 1074 "We have the function $f(x)=5sin⁡(2x).$ Notice that we are given the length $h$ of the interval. So, the average rate of change of $f$ over this interval is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h},\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Substituting into the formula and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+h)-f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5sin⁡(2(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+h))-5sin⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5sin⁡(\\pi +2h)-5sin⁡(\\pi )}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5sin⁡(\\pi +2h)-5(0)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5sin⁡(\\pi +2h)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{h}sin⁡(\\pi +2h).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186235424 1071 "![](images/47/47553a1d2bcd37d3040bf96c0fc0446b81404a07ab931e457788e62d86dee234.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\nThe graph of the function $g(h)$ is shown above, where\n\n$$\ng(h)=\\frac{5}{h}\\sin ⁡(\\pi +2h).\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, what is $\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)?$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                              