                          \n                                        \n$\\cdot \\sin ⁡(2)$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186235427 1071 "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{(x-1)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{(x-1)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\cdot sin⁡(2)$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186235427 1074 "Previously, we found that \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the product rule to the original limit, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{(x-1)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-3x+2} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}[\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}\\cdot f(x)] \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}5\\sin ⁡(2x) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 5\\sin ⁡(2\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =-5\\cdot \\sin ⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186235427 1074 "Previously, we found that \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the product rule to the original limit, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{(x-1)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-3x+2} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}[\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}\\cdot f(x)] \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}5sin⁡(2x) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 5sin⁡(2\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =-5\\cdot sin⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186235433 1074 "Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nWe apply the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f}{h}\\right) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)-f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x)}{[h(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})}^{′}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}}{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{16{\\sin }^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{16{\\sin }^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{8{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186235433 1074 "Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nWe apply the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f}{h}\\right) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)-f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x)}{[h(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})}^{′}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}}{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{16{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{16{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{8{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186235445 1074 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating $v(t)$ at $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ we have\n\n$$\n2{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=2{(-2)}^{2}=8.\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186235445 1074 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating $v(t)$ at $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ we have\n\n$$\n2{csc}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=2{(-2)}^{2}=8.\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186235446 1074 "Substituting $t=2\\pi $ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}v(t) & =\\lim_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}2{\\csc }^{2}⁡(t) \\\\  & =2{\\csc }^{2}⁡(2\\pi ) \\\\  & =2\\frac{1}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t.$\n\n![](images/36/361e8bf3b622fc2e73be712dae4ec5d189bd15eecbd33c9295db7365bcf71943.png)\n\nAs $t$ approaches $2\\pi $ from the left, the values of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t$ are negative and decrease without bound. \n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=-\\infty \n$$\n\nApplying the product and power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}2{\\csc }^{2}⁡(2\\pi ) & =\\lim_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}2\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}{\\csc }^{2}⁡(t) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {(\\lim_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t))}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying by the positive constant $2$ does not change the sign of the limit, so we have\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {(\\lim_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t))}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186235446 1074 "Substituting $t=2\\pi $ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}v(t) & =\\lim_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}2{csc}^{2}⁡(t) \\\\  & =2{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\pi ) \\\\  & =2\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t.$\n\n![](images/36/361e8bf3b622fc2e73be712dae4ec5d189bd15eecbd33c9295db7365bcf71943.png)\n\nAs $t$ approaches $2\\pi $ from the left, the values of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t$ are negative and decrease without bound. \n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=-\\infty \n$$\n\nA