c{3{x}^{2}-6}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2x}-\\frac{3}{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{3}{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}-3ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186238002 1074 "Expanding the binomial and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{3}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{9}{4{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{9}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x+3\\ln ⁡|x|+\\frac{9{x}^{-2+1}}{4(-2+1)}+C \\\\  & =x+3\\ln ⁡|x|-\\frac{9}{4x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=3\\ln ⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $3\\ln ⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{3}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{9}{4x}+3\\ln ⁡|x|+3\\ln ⁡K \\\\  & =x-\\frac{9}{4x}+3(\\ln ⁡|x|+\\ln ⁡K) \\\\  & =x-\\frac{9}{4x}+3\\ln ⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186238002 1074 "Expanding the binomial and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{3}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{9}{4{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{9}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x+3ln⁡|x|+\\frac{9{x}^{-2+1}}{4(-2+1)}+C \\\\  & =x+3ln⁡|x|-\\frac{9}{4x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=3ln⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $3ln⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{3}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{9}{4x}+3ln⁡|x|+3ln⁡K \\\\  & =x-\\frac{9}{4x}+3(ln⁡|x|+ln⁡K) \\\\  & =x-\\frac{9}{4x}+3ln⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186238028 1074 "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $10$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{10}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\ln ⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186238028 1074 "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $10$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{10}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186238030 1074 "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $2$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\ln ⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=2\\ln ⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $2\\ln ⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\ln ⁡|x|+2\\ln ⁡K \\\\  & =2(\\ln ⁡|x|+\\ln ⁡K) \\\\  & =2\\ln ⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186238030 1074 "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $2$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=2ln⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $2ln⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =2ln⁡|x|+2ln⁡K \\\\  & =2(ln⁡|x|+ln⁡K) \\\\  & =2ln⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186238035 1071 "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}{\\csc }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186238035 1071 "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}{csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186238035 1074 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}{\\csc }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x{|}_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)-(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =-(-\\sqrt{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186238035 1074 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}{csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x{|}_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)-(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =-(-\\sqrt{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186238041 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\sec ⁡x?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186238041 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=sec⁡x?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186238041 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\sec ⁡x.$\n\n![](ima