g signs.\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{\\det (A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-1)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186240804 1074 "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** We are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-1)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186240821 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $y=\\ln ⁡x+\\frac{1}{6{x}^{2}}.$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186240821 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $y=ln⁡x+\\frac{1}{6{x}^{2}}.$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186240821 1074 "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln ⁡x+\\frac{1}{6{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln ⁡x+\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-2){x}^{-2-1} \\\\  & ={x}^{-1}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-1}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =(-1){x}^{-1-1}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-3){x}^{-3-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186240821 1074 "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x+\\frac{1}{6{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x+\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-2){x}^{-2-1} \\\\  & ={x}^{-1}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-1}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =(-1){x}^{-1-1}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-3){x}^{-3-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186240822 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $y=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}+2\\sin ⁡x.$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186240822 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $y=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}+2sin⁡x.$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186240822 1074 "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}+2\\sin ⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{-1/2}+2\\sin ⁡x) \\\\  & =4\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){x}^{-1/2-1}+2\\cos ⁡x \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-3/2}+2\\cos ⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{x}^{-3/2}+2\\cos ⁡x) \\\\  & =-2\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right){x}^{-3/2-1}-2\\sin ⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}-2\\sin ⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186240822 1074 "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}+2sin⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{-1/2}+2sin⁡x) \\\\  & =4\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){x}^{-1/2-1}+2cos⁡x \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-3/2}+2cos⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{x}^{-3/2}+2cos⁡x) \\\\  & =-2\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right){x}^{-3/2-1}-2sin⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}-2sin⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186240837 1071 "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as \n$\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{r}^{4}}={\\sin }^{2}⁡2\\theta $\nfor $z\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ What is the third component of $f?$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186240837 1071 "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as \n$\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{r}^{4}}={sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta $\nfor $z\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ What is the third component of $f?$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186240837 1074 "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(r,\\theta ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{r}^{4}} & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡2\\theta  \\\\ {z}^{2} & ={r}^{4}{\\sin }^{2}⁡2\\theta  \\\\ z & ={r}^{2}|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta | \\\\ z & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that $z\\ge 0.$ Also, we drop the absolute value bars because $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\ge 0$ for  $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$  \n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $z={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is ${r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta .$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186240837 1074 "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(r,\\theta ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{r}^{4}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta  \\\\ {z}^{2} & ={r}^{4}{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta  \\\\ z & ={r}^{2}|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta | \\\\ z & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that $z\\ge 0.$ Also, we drop the absolute value bars because $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\ge 0$ for  $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$  \n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $z={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is ${r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta .$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186240845 1071 "Differentiate ${e}^{x}\\cos ⁡x.$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186240845 1071 "Differentiate ${e}^{x}cos⁡x.$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186240845 1074 "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\cos ⁡x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({e}^{x}\\cos ⁡x)^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})^{′}\\cdot \\cos ⁡x+{e}^{x}\\cdot (\\cos ⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(\\cos ⁡x)+({e}^{x})(-\\sin ⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cos ⁡x-{e}^{x}\\sin ⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186240845 1074 "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=cos⁡x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({e}^{x}cos⁡x)^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})^{′}\\cdot cos⁡x+{e}^{x}\\cdot (cos⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(cos⁡x)+({e}^{x})(-sin⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}cos⁡x-{e}^{x}sin⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186240848 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ if $y=\\frac{3\\sec ⁡x}{{e}^{x}}.$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186240848 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ if $y=\\frac{3sec⁡x}{{e}^{x}}.$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186240848 1074 "We calculate the derivative using the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{3\\sec ⁡x}{{e}^{x}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(3\\sec ⁡x\\tan ⁡x)({e}^{x})-(3\\sec ⁡x)({e}^{x})}{({e}^{x})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{x}\\sec ⁡x(\\tan ⁡x-1)}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sec ⁡x(\\tan ⁡x-1)}{{e}^{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186240848 1074 "We calculate the derivative using the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{3sec⁡x}{{e}^{x}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(3sec⁡xtan⁡x)({e}^{x})-(3sec⁡x)({e}^{x})}{({e}^{x})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{x}sec⁡x(tan⁡x-1)}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3sec⁡x(tan⁡x-1)}{{e}^{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186240858 1071 "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,ϕ{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as \n$\\rho =ϕ{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the first component of $f?$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186240858 1071 "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as \n$\\rho =ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the first compo