 function $f$ has a local maximum at $(5,-4).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,0)$ and a local maximum at $(5,-4).$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186241041 1074 "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4-4y-4x & 4-4x+{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4-4y-4x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4-4x+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4-4y-4x &=0 \\\\ 4-4x+{y}^{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n4-4y-4x=0\\,⟹\\,x=1-y.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-4(1-y)+{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+4y & =0 \\\\ y(y+4) & =0 \\\\ y & =-4,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $x=1-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny=-4\\,⟹\\,x=1-(-4)=5,\\,\\,y=0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,x=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(1,0)$ and $(5,-4)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-4y-4x)=-4\\, & \\frac{{∂