le. Since the matrix is not square, we can't say that it's singular.\n\nTherefore, only $H$ and $J$ is singular." 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186253311 1074 "- For the matrix $H$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(H)=5\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 0=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(H)=0,$ we conclude that $H$ is not invertible. Since $H$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $H$ is singular.\n\n- For the matrix $J$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(J)=2\\cdot 6-3\\cdot 4=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(J)=0,$ we conclude that $J$ is not invertible. Since $J$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $J$ is singular.\n\n- The matrix $K$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. So, it is not invertible. Since the matrix is not square, we can't say that it's singular.\n\nTherefore, only $H$ and $J$ is singular." 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186253312 1074 "The matrix $F$ is singular if and only if $\\det (F)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (F) & =3\\cdot 3-t\\cdot t \\\\  & =9-{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =9 \\\\ t & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the values of $t$ are $t=\\pm 3.$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186253312 1074 "The matrix $F$ is singular if and only if $det(F)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(F) & =3\\cdot 3-t\\cdot t \\\\  & =9-{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =9 \\\\ t & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the values of $t$ are $t=\\pm 3.$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186253313 1074 "The matrix $Q$ is singular if and only if $\\det (Q)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (Q) & =3\\cdot x-x\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =3x-6x \\\\  & =-3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $x$ is $x=0.$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186253313 1074 "The matrix $Q$ is singular if and only if $det(Q)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q) & =3\\cdot x-x\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =3x-6x \\\\  & =-3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $x$ is $x=0.$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186253320 1074 "The matrix $P$ is singular if and only if $\\det (P)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det (P) & =n\\cdot n-(-2)\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & ={n}^{2}-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-16 & =0 \\\\ {n}^{2} & =16 \\\\ n & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the values of $n$ are $n=\\pm 4.$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186253320 1074 "The matrix $P$ is singular if and only if $det(P)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P) & =n\\cdot n-(-2)\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & ={n}^{2}-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-16 & =0 \\\\ {n}^{2} & =16 \\\\ n & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the values of $n$ are $n=\\pm 4.$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186253325 1074 "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{x}^{a}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\nThe integrand ${e}^{1-{t}^{2}}$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying the FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-{x}^{2}}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x){e}^{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-{x}^{2}}-{e}^{1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-{x}^{2}}-{e}^{{\\sin }^{2}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${f}^{′}(0).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-{e}^{1-{0}^{2}}-{e}^{{\\sin }^{2}⁡0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-0}-0 \\\\  & =-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186253325 1074 "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{x}^{a}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\nThe integrand ${e}^{1-{t}^{2}}$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying the FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-{x}^{2}}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x){e}^{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-{x}^{2}}-{e}^{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-{x}^{2}}-{e}^{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${f}^{′}(0).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-{e}^{1-{0}^{2}}-{e}^{{sin}^{2}⁡0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-0}-0 \\\\  & =-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186253472 1074 "![](images/6b/6ba976f250811d1ba3109f4aecbfdb2a4de4ee86300fa867e80e2087aa21601f.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\sin ⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-\\sin ⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $\\frac{7}{4}$ to give $y=-\\frac{7}{4}\\sin ⁡x.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-\\frac{7}{4}\\sin ⁡x.$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186253472 1074 "![](images/6b/6ba976f250811d1ba3109f4aecbfdb2a4de4ee86300fa867e80e2087aa21601f.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=sin⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-sin⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $\\frac{7}{4}$ to give $y=-\\frac{7}{4}sin⁡x.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph