nsider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2).$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2))^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{\\ln }^{2}⁡(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2} & =0 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)}{x-2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186253477 1074 "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2).$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2))^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2} & =0 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)}{x-2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186253480 1074 "![](images/84/8499856915baa0bf144561dd35143e024586f4d417ac4f9c1ee48d7586e0f26c.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\sin ⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-\\sin ⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, translate it by $1$ unit **down** to give $y=-\\sin ⁡x-1.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-\\sin ⁡x-1.$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186253480 10