th sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {\\sec }^{2}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {\\sec }^{2}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {\\sec }^{2}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡y & ={e}^{x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡y={e}^{x}+C.\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186253525 1074 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x}{cos}^{2}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {sec}^{2}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡y & ={e}^{x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡y={e}^{x}+C.\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186253529 1071 "Given that $y=5{\\log }_{4}⁡x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186253529 1071 "Given that $y=5{log}_{4}⁡x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186253529 1074 "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\log }_{a}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x\\ln ⁡a}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{\\log }_{4}⁡x) \\\\  & =5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\log }_{4}⁡x) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\ln ⁡4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{x\\ln ⁡4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186253529 1074 "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{xln⁡a}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{log}_{4}⁡x) \\\\  & =5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{4}⁡x) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{xln⁡4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{xln⁡4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186253538 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{2}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{2}^{x})\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\left(\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\right)+Cx+D \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{x}}{{\\ln }^{2}⁡2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186253538 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{2}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, integratin