 that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n2C=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186275139 1074 "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{\\sin }^{2}⁡u+{\\cos }^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{\\cos }^{2}⁡u=1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\cos }^{2}⁡u & =1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{15}{17}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{8}{17}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{45}{85}-\\frac{32}{85} \\\\  & =-\\frac{77}{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [17592186275139 1074 "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorna