e the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡y} \\\\ {sec}^{2}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡y & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡y=x+C.\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 false] [17592186275347 1074 "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we plug $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right){(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),$ ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),$ and ${f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x) & ⟹ &  & f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡(x)} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{\\cos }^{3}⁡(x)} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),$ ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),$ and ${f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =1+2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =1+2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545672 17592186576411 true] [1